    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d630">

                <pb facs="0682" n="670" xml:id="pg670"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/24470: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-09-24T18:00:00-03:00">September 24, 1942—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:34 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3800. Department’s 2807, September 23, 9 p.m.<anchor n="74" xml:id="d630-f1"/>
                    Aranha is under the impression that there have been set up at Washington
                    councils and boards consequent upon the declaration. He asked me what they
                    are.</p>
                <p>He repeated that Brazil has no interest in the United Nations; but a great deal
                    of interest in cooperating with us. “If you want us to sign” he said, “we will
                    sign. Should this be done with some formality; and where. Spell out to me
                    exactly what you want us to do.”</p>
                <p>(This is a typical Aranha sort of move.)</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d630-f1" n="74" xml:id="d630-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in this telegram the Ambassador was asked as to Brazil’s adherence
                    to the United Nations Declaration. For Aranha’s initial viewpoint, see telegram
                    No. 3250, August 26, 5 p.m., from the Chargé in Brazil, <ref target="#pg667">p.
                        667</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
